  "int5_prior_retest_verdict": "EXISTENCE-PROOF CROSS-CHECK applied. The int5 cap (S~0.49 @ d_seg~0.0035) was treated as a SUSPECT abs-max recipe artifact, NOT a floor, and re-tested with the canonical low-bit OUTLIER-HANDLING fix (mse_calib). Result: the fix is REAL but PARTIAL \u2014 mse_calib int5 S=0.459 (d_seg 0.00261) beats absmax int5 S=0.634 (d_seg 0.00402), matching the sister int5-LSQ-best-shot (S~0.486, d_seg~0.00266). So the abs-max number WAS partly a recipe artifact (mse_calib/LSQ improves it), BUT the FLOOR is real: even the best codec-byte-closeable PTQ fix leaves int5 at S=0.459 = 2.4x the frontier 0.191, d_seg 4.6x the floor. The deeper fix (per-channel scales) is NOT byte-closeable through the per-tensor-int8 codec grammar (measured: 118k->197k). The sister int5 QAT-FINETUNE best-shot confirmed the d_seg wall holds under training (-9.5% d_seg only). int5 PTQ cap CONFIRMED (refined): a real byte-closed floor, partly recipe-improvable, never sub-0.19.",
  "ptq_vs_qat_caveat": "PTQ = $0 LOWER BOUND. QAT-finetune is the real version; the int5 best-shot already ran it and recovered d_pose -89% but d_seg only -9.5% (wall held). The d_seg-critical early/low-res stages (77% of params) need finer-than-int-N per-channel resolution no per-tensor scale provides AND per-channel is not byte-closeable. Open follow-up: QAT-finetune at int6/int7 (smaller PTQ d_seg damage, more finetune-recoverable) \u2014 but PTQ int6 already S=0.295 (mse), so even full d_seg recovery to the int8 d_seg would leave S ~= 0.19 (int6 bytes 160977 -> rate 0.107 + int8 d_seg 0.0006 + pose ~ 0.155, barely at the frontier, NOT sub-0.15)."
